            <div type="intro">
                <p>Introduction to the Passage from the <hi rend="italic">Sutra on the Great
                        Perfection of Wisdom</hi> on the 18 Aspects of Emptiness</p>
                <p>The passage is drawn from the Kumārajīva translation of the <hi rend="italic">Mahā-prajñā-pāramitā sūtra</hi>, T#223, 8: 250b2-7. The Kuchean translator
                    Kumārajīva arrived in China in the year 401, where he arguably became the single
                    most influential translator in the history of East Asian Buddhism. His
                    translations include versions of the <hi rend="italic">Lotus Sutra</hi> and the
                        <hi rend="italic">Diamond Sutra</hi>. The received version in <hi rend="italic">Taishō</hi> version differs slightly, reading “This
                    constitutes the Mahāyāna of the bodhisattva. And then, Subhuti, the
                    bodhisattva-mahāsattva also has a Mahāyāna . . .”. The Mount Hongding text
                    begins with the last sentence of the previous section within the source text,
                    which it seems to present as a subject line of the text as a whole. The <hi rend="italic">Treatise on the Great Perfection of Wisdom</hi> commentary on
                    the passage is located at T#1509, 25: 393b-c.</p>
            </div>
            <div xml:lang="en">
                <head>
                    <title level="a" type="sutra" xlink:href="T_223"> “Mahayana Sutra” </title>
                </head>
                <div>
                    <p>The Mahayana of the bodhisattva—furthermore, <persName corresp="zh-a1" key="rh0002" reg="Subhuti" type="disciple of Buddha" xml:id="en-a1">
                            <foreign xml:lang="sa">Subhūti</foreign>
                        </persName>, the Mahayana
                        of the <hi rend="italic">
                            <foreign xml:lang="sa">bodhisattva-mahāsattva</foreign>
                        </hi>—[consists of] what is called
                        the emptiness of the internal, the emptiness of the external, the emptiness
                        of what is both internal and external, the emptiness of emptiness, the great
                        emptiness, the emptiness of the supreme truth, the emptiness of the
                        conditioned, the emptiness of the unconditioned, the emptiness of the final,
                        the emptiness of the beginningless, the emptiness of the analyzed, the
                        emptiness of [intrinsic] nature, the emptiness of self attributes, the
                        emptiness of all entities, the emptiness of non-obtainability, the emptiness
                        of non-existent entities, the emptiness of existent entities, [and] the
                        emptiness of entities that are both existent and non-existent. </p>
                </div>
            </div>
